The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{5x^3+2x^2-7}{x^4+3x}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5x^3+2x^2-7}{x^4}}{\dfrac{x^4+3x}{x^4}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^4}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5\cancel{x^3}}{x\cdot\cancel{x^3}}+\dfrac{2\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}\cdot x^2}-\dfrac{7}{x^4}}{\dfrac{\cancel{x^4}}{\cancel{x^4}}+\dfrac{3\cancel{x}}{x^3\cdot\cancel{x}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{5}{x}+\dfrac{2}{x^2}-\dfrac{7}{x^4}}{1+\dfrac{3}{x^3}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0+0-0}{1+0}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{1}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$
